preferred-citation:
  type: article
  authors:
    - family-names: "Büth"
      given-names: "Carlson Moses"
      orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2298-8438"
  doi: "10.3390/e28121309"
  url: "https://www.mdpi.com/1099-4300/28/12/1309"
  title: "infomeasure-rs: A High-Performance Rust Library for Information-Theoretic Measures and Estimators"
  year: 2026
  month: 11
  journal: "Entropy"
  volume: 28
  issue: 12
  start: 1309
  abstract: |
    Information theory has become a standard tool across the natural and social
    sciences, yet practical use is limited by estimator choice, fragmented
    software, reproducibility, and computational cost. We present
    infomeasure-rs, an open-source Rust library that brings a broad set of
    information-theoretic measures and estimators into one self-contained,
    type-safe package. It covers entropy, mutual information, conditional
    mutual information, transfer entropy, conditional transfer entropy,
    cross-entropy, and the Kullback-Leibler and Jensen-Shannon divergences, with
    discrete (plug-in and bias-corrected), kernel, ordinal, and
    nearest-neighbour estimators, including the Rényi and Tsallis entropies, and
    exposes local and global values and significance testing through a
    consistent trait interface. It compiles to self-contained native code with
    no language runtime dependency and is continuously validated against a
    reference implementation. On a single central processing unit (CPU) core,
    and even without its GPU or parallel paths, it is the fastest of the
    packages we compared across languages, matching the closest packages only
    for plug-in discrete entropy and substantially faster for mutual
    information, the conditional and transfer measures, and the kernel, ordinal,
    and generalised-entropy estimators. The optional GPU and multi-threaded
    paths accelerate selected estimators further. The library is open source
    and, to our knowledge, the most complete information-theory package for
    Rust.
